        "fixed_by_detail": "r_c floor = 5 a_mean is a JUDGEMENT, not a measured floor. Convergence was checked 5a -> 7a AT the A=100 production point, Gamma = 29.7. The run MEASURES a hexagonal crystal there -- psi_6 = 0.885 and six-fold coordination 98.6%, both from the trajectory (runs/soft-r3-2d-A-sweep__A100__30caa5c9e0). Structure moved 0.15%, <U>/N moved +7.5%. ⚠ `state_predicted = 결정` is NOT one of those measurements, and an earlier draft listed it as if it were: it comes from `approach_distance(A, a_star, eps)`, a function of the INPUTS only, which never touches the trajectory. It is an estimator's label that happens to agree. Corrected 2026-09-11. ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-11. An earlier draft of this line called Gamma = 29.7 \"deep in the liquid\" and set it against the hexatic window \"Gamma 55.87-59.88\". That mixed two Gamma conventions: Zahn's is Gamma = pi^1.5 * beta U(a_mean) -- Zahn's `d` is the MEAN SPACING n^(-1/2) = L/sqrt(N), NOT the particle diameter, which is what `d` means everywhere else in this file (5 um). Writing it as `beta U(d)` was a third error in this sentence, caught 2026-09-11. This file's convention is Gamma = U(a_mean)/kT, so the window here is 10.03-10.75 and Gamma = 29.7 sits 2.8x ABOVE it, not below it in the liquid (simbot/analysis/structure.py ZAHN_GAMMA_OVER_A = pi^1.5; findings/order-parameter-magnitude-cannot-identify-a-phase already records the conversion). Same class of error the system card warns about: confusing two length conventions by 7% makes Gamma 23% wrong. The conclusion survives the correction but for a different reason: the check was run in the crystal, far above the window on the other side, so it still says nothing about cutoff sensitivity AT the transition. Combined with r_c < L/2 this gives sqrt(N)/2 > 9 -> N > 324 -> 400.",
